  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Tampa Bay Rays opened the season with a split personality, winning their April series on the backs of clutch contributions from catcher Nick Fortes while their bullpen — particularly Bryan Baker — showed early vulnerability. Still, the Rays surged through April at 16-9, establishing themselves as a legitimate American League contender powered by a balanced roster and a pitching staff that kept runs off the board at an elite rate.\n\nMay and June tested that identity. A Daulton Varsho grand slam off Aaron Brooks represented the season's single most damaging play and threatened to unravel a team riding a hot streak, and a 13-13 June raised real questions about whether Tampa Bay could sustain its pace. But the Rays never truly collapsed — veterans like Yandy Díaz and young stars like Junior Caminero and Jonathan Aranda kept delivering in the biggest moments, preventing any prolonged freefall and keeping the club well above .500 through the summer's toughest stretch.\n\nThe final two months were where this team announced itself. August and September brought a combined 34-19 record, fueled by Jonny DeLuca's clutch home run power, Junior Caminero's historic 41-homer campaign, and a pitching staff that suffocated opponents down the stretch. The Rays finished 98-64 — a statement season by any measure — and dispatched their ALDS opponent behind postseason contributions from unlikely heroes like Victor Mesa Jr. and Ryan Vilade, who stepped up on the October stage.\n\nNow the Rays find themselves alive in the postseason, having survived a tense ALDS in which Bryan Baker's relief work and late-inning grit proved the difference. Junior Caminero, Aranda, and a bullpen that has steadied itself after a rocky regular season are carrying Tampa Bay forward as the October chase continues, with the franchise chasing its deepest postseason run in years.",
